{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>For honor and for splendor</b> — that he be honored and glorified in dignified and splendid garments, as Scripture says, \"As a bridegroom priests in glory\" (Isaiah 61:10). For these garments are royal vestments; kings in the time of the Torah would dress in their likeness, as we find regarding the tunic: \"And he made him a tunic of stripes\" (Genesis 37:3), which means an embroidered garment in the likeness of stripes (פַסִּים), and it is a tunic of brocade as I explained (Ramban on Exodus 25:7), and they clothed him like the son of kings of old. So too the manner of the cloak and tunic: \"Now upon her was a tunic of stripes, for thus would the king's virgin daughters wear cloaks\" (II Samuel 13:18), which means that the tunic of stripes upon her was visible and exposed, for the custom was that the king's virgin daughters would wear cloaks in which they would be veiled, so that the tunic of stripes upon her was the outer garment, and therefore it says, \"And the tunic of stripes that was upon her he tore\" (ibid. v. 19).\n\n<b>The mitre</b> (מִצְנֶפֶת) is known even today among kings and great princes, and therefore Scripture says concerning the fall of the monarchy, \"Remove the mitre and lift off the crown\" (Ezekiel 21:31), and likewise it is written, \"a diadem of royalty\" (Isaiah 62:3). And thus Scripture calls them \"the splendor of the turbans\" (Exodus 39:28), and it is written, \"splendorous turbans of linen shall be upon their heads\" (Ezekiel 44:18) — they are splendor and glory for those who bind them. And the ephod and the breastpiece are royal attire, as in the matter written: \"and the chain of gold about your neck\" (Daniel 5:16). The frontlet (צִיץ) is the crown of kings, as it is written (Psalms 132:18), \"His crown will sprout (יָצִיץ)\" — and the enlightened will understand. They are of gold, purple, and blue, as it is written (Psalms 45:14), \"All glorious is the king's daughter within; her clothing is of gold settings inset,\" and it is written (Daniel 5:16), \"You shall wear purple and the chain of gold about your neck.\" And blue (תְכֵלֶת) — even today no man would lift his hand to wear it except a king of the nations, as it is written (Esther 8:15), \"And Mordecai went out from before the king in royal apparel of blue and white, with a great golden crown and a wrapping of fine linen and purple.\" The wrapping (תַכְרִיךְ) is the cloak (מְעִיל) in which he wraps himself.\n\nAnd by way of truth, <b>for honor and for splendor</b> — he says to make holy garments for Aharon to minister in them for the honor of the Name that dwells among them and for the splendor of His might, as it is written (Psalms 89:18), \"For You are the splendor of their might,\" and it is written (Isaiah 64:10), \"Our holy and beautiful house, where our fathers praised You\" — and \"our holy\" is the honor, and \"our beautiful\" is the splendor of Yisrael. And further it is said (Psalms 96:6), \"Strength and splendor are in His sanctuary,\" and likewise, \"to beautify the place of My sanctuary, and the place of My feet I will honor\" (Isaiah 60:13) — that the place of the sanctuary shall be beautified in splendor, and the place of His feet, which is the site of the Temple, honored with the honor of the Name. And likewise, \"and in Yisrael He will be glorified\" (Isaiah 44:23) — that He will show and unite His splendor among them. And so He says further on, regarding all the garments of the sons as well, \"for honor and for splendor\" (Exodus 28:40), and He says concerning the offerings, \"They shall go up with acceptance upon My altar, and My house of splendor I will beautify\" (Isaiah 60:7). Now the altar is His acceptance, and the honor is His house of splendor.\n\nAnd the garments require craftsmanship for their making, and perhaps they also require intention, and therefore He said, <b>And you shall speak to all who are wise of heart, whom I have filled with the spirit of wisdom</b> (Exodus 28:3) — that they may understand what they are to make. And the Sages have already said (Yoma 72b): His likeness battles for me in the house of my war."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "The word תַכְרִיךְ in Esther 8:15 (here vocalized as תַכְרִיךְ rather than the more common תַּכְרִיךְ) is explained by Ramban as the מְעִיל; I render it literally as 'wrapping.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:19:34.181150140Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}